# paq8-T1.idx -- State_table columns [2] and [3]: the bit-history counts.
#
# State_table[s] = { next0, next1, n0, n1 }.  This file holds the last two --
# how many 0s and 1s the state stands for -- and paq8-T0.idx holds the
# transitions.  Split so that this half can be swept while the other half stays
# folded: T0 says `Const 1` in its own source, so mk.sh's release rewrite of
# `^Const 0` never reaches it and it is literals in both builds, while this file
# is a live pdesc pattern per value in the tuning build.
#
# So `./mk.sh` gives you Const 0 for the counts only, which is what these are
# separated for.
#
# WHAT MOVING THESE DOES
#
# n0/n1 are read in exactly two places and both are about EVIDENCE, not about
# control flow:
#
#   StateMap()   the per-state prior, PR_ONE*(n1+ADD)/(n0+n1+2*ADD), with the
#                pure side boosted 128x -- what a state predicts before it has
#                been seen in this context
#   mix2t        as -!n0 and -!n1, i.e. the all-ones masks for "no 0s observed"
#                and "no 1s observed", which gate the two one-sided confidence
#                terms that only fire on deterministic histories
#
# So a swept value changes a prior and can flip a state between "deterministic"
# and "mixed".  Neither can crash anything or change which state follows which,
# which is why this half is the safe half to hand an optimizer.
#
# Six bits: the counts saturate around 41 in the shipped table and the pattern
# reaches 63.  A count of 0 is meaningful (it is what the -!n tests look for),
# so unlike a mask there is no reason to floor it; the one division it feeds is
# guarded at the point of use, per IDX-FORMAT.md section 5.
#
# 253 rows, matching the array literal; rows 253..255 stay implicitly zero.

Prefix T1
Debug 1
Const 0

Number s000_c0, 1, 0!000000
Number s000_c1, 1, 0!000000
Number s001_c0, 1, 0!000001
Number s001_c1, 1, 0!000000
Number s002_c0, 1, 0!000000
Number s002_c1, 1, 0!000001
Number s003_c0, 1, 0!000010
